On 2006-10-16, BandC <onefunus@yahoo.com> wrote:
> 	I'm sure this has been asked before but I haven't seen anything lately 
> so I'll ask. How do people in this newsgroup feel about Slash 'em? 

When I have a good nethack game going but don't want to kill it, I roll
up a Slash'EM character and kill it. Occasionally, it takes me a long time
to kill it. I like to think of Slash'EM as an unstable branch of Nethack;
lots of features get added and cause the game to be unbalanced (in both
directions; there are more things that kill you but there are more things
you can use to kill things), and then Nethack absorbs and carefully
balances the good ones. Someone else mentioned Nethack+, which I think
served the same role (isn't that where the spell skill system debuted?).

I actually like that Slash'EM tries to muck with the balance a bit; Magic
Mapping as level 7, for example. means that you can't just shortcut the
mazes with every character, at least not without burning a whole lot
of scrolls. Maybe that's not a good idea* but it's being playtested in
Slash'EM and I think that is cool. Of course, sometimes there are so many
unbalancing things that it's hard to tell whether an individual change is
good or not.

I wouldn't be surprised to see some things in the next major version of
Nethack that are borrowed from Slash'EM or other patches. At the top of
my personal wishlist are:

 * the fun one-level dungeon branches that are optional, difficult, 
   and rewarding
 * artifact armor, or at least Monks getting something less stupid when
   sacrificing and getting crowned
 * more random named monsters you might or might not encounter

Of course, some Slash'EM features are at the _bottom_ of my wishlist, too :)
"In that sense, at least, it is pretty balanced"

